<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bianca Maria Visconti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1425–<a href="/years/1468">1468</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">House of Sforza</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bianca Maria Visconti was the only child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-maria-visconti">Filippo Maria Visconti</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, born to his mistress. Her marriage to the condottiere <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a> in 1441 gave him a claim to Milan, which he took as duke in 1450. As duchess she shared in government and acted as regent for her son Galeazzo Maria after Sforza's death in 1466.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bianca-maria-visconti.jpg" alt="Bianca Maria Visconti" width="320" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bonifacio Bembo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bianca_Maria_Visconti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bianca Maria Visconti was born on 31 March 1425 near Settimo Pavese, the illegitimate daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-maria-visconti">Filippo Maria Visconti</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and last ruler of the Visconti dynasty, and Agnese del Maino, a lady-in-waiting to his wife Beatrice di Tenda. At six months she was sent with her mother to a castle at Abbiate, where the duke often visited and was reportedly struck by her strong character. She grew up at Abbiategrasso with a humanist education.</p><p>In 1430, aged five, she was betrothed to the condottiero Francesco I Sforza, twenty-four years older, to keep him tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> after his contract ended. The contract was signed on 23 February 1432 and her dowry included lands around Cremona. She married Sforza on 24 October 1441 in Cremona.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1442, aged seventeen, she was named regent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> while Sforza fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-piccinino">Niccolò Piccinino</a>, and chroniclers record her skill in administration and diplomacy. Filippo Maria died in the night of 13 to 14 August 1447, and Sforza took the post of captain general of the Ambrosian Republic. In May 1448, when Venetians attacked Cremona, she is said to have put on parade armour and fought on the walls for a whole day, which earned her fame as a warrior woman. On 24 February 1450 a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> recognised Sforza as lord, the first time a duke's title was given by a city's citizens.</p><p>As duchess she ran the duchy while Sforza was at war, and their letters show her opinions when they differed from his. After the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> in 1454 she turned to diplomacy, restoration of ducal residences and public works, among them the Ospedale Maggiore. When Sforza fell ill in 1462 she acted as co-regent and kept the state from crumbling after rebellions encouraged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sforza died on 8 March 1466. Bianca Maria took the regency and called back their son Galeazzo Maria from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to succeed. He soon acted against her advice and forced her to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> for Cremona, her dower city. She attended his wedding on 9 May 1468, fell ill at Melegnano and died on 28 October 1468. She was buried in the Duomo of Milan beside her husband, with a funeral oration by the humanist Francesco Filelfo. Bartolomeo Colleoni and others accused Galeazzo of poisoning her, though Bernardino Corio wrote that she died of natural ungratefulness more than poison. Of her ten children, Galeazzo Maria and Ludovico Maria became dukes of Milan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bianca Maria Visconti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the death of Filippo Maria in 1447 without a legitimate son, citizens proclaimed the Golden Ambrosian Republic, but it collapsed in 1450 and Francesco Sforza, husband of Filippo Maria’s daughter <b>Bianca Maria</b>, entered Milan as duke.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Threatened by the Republic of Venice, it hired Francesco Sforza, husband of the late duke's illegitimate daughter <b>Bianca Maria</b>, who defeated the Venetians at Caravaggio in 1448.</blockquote><a href="/state/golden-ambrosian-republic">Golden Ambrosian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bornasco, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Cremona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.27,9.22&amp;year=1452" aria-label="Bornasco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4769 566 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bianca Maria Visconti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5219 836h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5219 836h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.27,9.22&amp;year=1452">What was here in 1452? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
